a bunch of questions but uh i like  asking directors a curveball at the  beginning um if you could get the  financing to make anything you want what  would you make and why i'm probably the  wrong person to ask this because  i don't have uh  as of right now i don't have aspirations  to tell a  massive tentpole story um i i  i tend to focus on stuff that  um feels very character driven and kind  of dissect and analyze character and so  um  budget for me  is less of an issue because i i tend to  tell stories of people talking in rooms  and so the the budgets are quite low but  um  yeah i i don't i don't have a i don't  have an answer to that specifically i i  think for me if i'm able to continue  telling  thought-provoking stories that um that  allows an audience to  think for themselves and have their own  ideas that's that's what i like to do  you know so whether that  is is a much bigger budget film or or a  contained smaller film  um  that  it doesn't really matter to me it's all  about  exploring ideas and characters within  those ideas  uh in the in the credits you thank james  gray and david benioff um and i'm just  curious why those two there was other  people but you're really you're really  analyzing nice i i didn't think anyone  stays and stays for the full credits um  i  i have gotten to know both of of them a  little bit um and  i i mean i'm i'm a huge fan of of both  of their work and i feel like whenever i  make a film  um  i try to reach out to in my mind  the most talented  interesting minds i can to get their  feedback  um it terrifies me  sending the film to james gray terrifies  me um because i have such respect for  him and he's so brutally honest  but i really wanted his notes i really  wanted his thoughts and and if he could  give me one note that helped the film  that that i wanted that so  um for both of those guys uh i sent this  film and and was able to have a  conversation with them and yeah it was  it was really special  did you actually uh i i know a bunch of  directors and i i know  a lot of people do what you did with  that in terms of showing it to people  and getting honest feedback did you make  any big changes as a result of feedback  from friends and family  i wouldn't say i made any big changes  mostly because with this film  you have a  a very structured story that you can't  you can't change scenes around it it's  the first time i've ever made a film  where i'm like oh  this scene that i thought was gonna go  here i'll move it ten minutes earlier um  i've done that quite a few times this  film is is so much about in real time in  the process of the situation  so i i don't i didn't make any big  sweeping changes but i got a lot of  really strong  character notes which is is so much of  the dna of this film so i feel like it  really improved by by showing directors  especially because  there were some really interesting ideas  about character  about performance you know do you have  this um and and so i i was really able  to  to get in there and then also  the way in which i shot it um it was  really pre-planned so  there's there's moments where i'm going  back to a visual that in the beginning  of the film represented something that  was you know beautiful and pure and  innocent and then i come back to that  same shot  an hour later and and there's a very  different feeling even though it's the  same  it's the same house it's the same shot  but now all of a sudden the  the place is corrupt and feels tainted  and scary and  so the idea  of showing it to other directors and  getting their feedback to see if that  connected with them  um that was really special to to have  conversations with those guys uh i love  the use of three amigos in the movie  because it is  a a great movie and a great clip but it  also says a lot about those three  characters and what this movie is about  thematically there's a yeah it's a very  good clip how tough was it to get the  rights to use it uh and what were you  going to do if you couldn't get the  rights i i feel like a lot of directors  would have this answer which is  your my mind didn't go till we're not  getting the rights it's like we have to  get the rights you know because one once  that clip the idea of that clip  came into our minds it was like okay  this is  this is the perfect clip thematically as  to what's sort of going on and what  we're exploring in the themes but also  the idea that there's a comedic  kind of  and then also a nod to an era of film  that i grew up on you know i i i watched  that film a hundred times over as a kid  so um but the idea that it's not like a  dramatic scene that's like that that's  trying to really point at this  this theme of have and have nots you  know um  but that there's comedy to it and and  that the characters  would actually laugh in this moment even  though they're in this situation  and then  in that moment jesse's character clocks  that that lily and jason are both  laughing and sort of connecting in some  way  and then that takes the movie into a  different direction like  tying in a a seemingly just funny clip  into something bigger  um  i just loved that idea so  so i i in my mind it was only that clip  um and uh  it it took a minute to to get the rights  to it and many conversations but um  but we we rejoiced when when the the  news came in that all the three actors  signed off and they were we were able to  do it  uh i'm out of time but i just want to  ask you something else real quick just  if i can't yeah  um so uh i love movies that don't answer  all the questions and obviously jason's  character  nobody is you know you never establish  who he is why he's doing this but in  your mind did you have those answers or  did you love the idea that it could be  just anything uh in the back of my mind  i have answers so i'm grounded in some  sort of reality you know but but the  idea of  um the audience  bringing in their own thoughts and ideas  into the film was always how i wanted to  tell it i never wanted to  spoon feed the audience or manipulate  them to feel a certain way about a  character  they're all flawed characters  there isn't a traditional protagonist we  sort of connect and disconnect with  characters throughout the film and so  you know if you believe that  jason is robbing this house for pure  reasons or  you know to pay for a parent's  surgery you know then then then you  should believe that that that's right  you know or if you believe that he's  just as corrupt as  as ceo then then you should believe that  you know so that was always the  intention but  um but yeah in  in the back of my mind i have ideas  which could also completely differ from  the other writers you know we all have  our own ideas coming into it  on that note gotta go congrats on the  movie hope you're making thanksgiving  real soon thank you thanks man  appreciate it
 

